// The host cogs: the two that run on every sample the driver publishes, and the
// session that decides what the machine is doing at all.
//
// The driver publishes one pose sample per cycle, and that stream is the clock
// the control-rate cogs here run on. Neither of them is time-triggered: a cog
// woken by a timer would drift against the bus cycle it is supposed to answer,
// and a cog woken by the sample cannot. Both are sized to see every sample
// rather than the latest one, because a pose series with a hole in it is not a
// series.
//
// The session is the exception, and for the opposite reason: it decides at
// human rates -- a script arrives, a sequence steps, a watchdog expires -- and
// running it on the sample stream would wake it fifty times a second to answer
// nothing. So it is woken by the message classes it acts on and by a lapse that
// floors its rate, and it reads the sample stream without being triggered by
// it.
//
// Every signal below is `multi_instance`, here and in `sim.clk`. That marker
// says an offboard consumer should expect more than one reporter of the
// quantity, and here it is a fact about the build rather than about the
// machine: the compiler builds a casing for every box a generating module
// declares *and* one for the system target over it, and each casing registers
// an instance of every signal its cogs carry. A single-instance signal in that
// arrangement refuses to compile. There is one of each cog in this system
// whatever the marker says, so a consumer aggregating one of these counters
// across "instances" is asking a question with a wrong answer available.
// TODO(clockwork-single-instance-signal)
//
// Five modules are imported under aliases because a cog or the box below
// carries a member of the same name: the session's script input is called
// `script` and its state slot `sess`, and the box's members are `mover`, `pose`
// and `pose_state`. A member name shadows a module name in the enclosing scope,
// which the compiler refuses as a redefinition.
// (A comment may not sit directly above a `use`, which is why this one is here.)
//
// Every channel here declares the schema it carries. A cog cannot tell a
// socket-fed channel from a simulator-fed one either way: what crosses a socket
// is the blob bytes of the same schema, so the parse path is the same code and
// the type says what the bytes are.

#![generate(cpp, cpp_cog, cpp_test_cog)]
#![cpp(namespace=brenn_reachy::motion)]

use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] cogs::config;
use cogs::mover as mover_vocab;
use cogs::pose_state as pose_state_vocab;
use cogs::schedule;
use cogs::script as script_vocab;
use cogs::session as session_vocab;
use cogs::session_cmd;
use driver::goal as goal_vocab;
use driver::health;
use driver::pose as pose_vocab;
use motion::faults;
use motion::timeline;
use @clockwork::std::signals::{ReportGroupPolicy, ReportingStrategy,
    ReportGroupLogType};

// The driver's sample stream: one message per bus cycle, always, whether or
// not the read succeeded. One publisher -- the driver -- as every channel in
// this repo's systems has.
//
// Sixteen messages deep against the deepest view on it, which is eight: a
// subscriber that fell far enough behind to be lapped would have been stalled
// for 320 ms at the control rate, and the driver's own dead-man de-torques the
// machine long before that.
channel DriverPose
{
    message_type: Tachyon<pose_vocab::PoseSample>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// Where the head is, one message per sample.
channel Estimates
{
    message_type: Tachyon<pose_vocab::PoseEstimate>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// The goal stream: what the machine is being asked to hold next, one message
// per sample while a session is under way. One publisher -- the decision tick.
//
// Sixteen deep against the deepest view on it, which is eight, for the same
// reason the sample channel is: a subscriber lapped on this channel has been
// stalled long past the driver's own dead-man.
//
// The stream is a keep-alive as well as a command. A holding machine
// re-publishes the setpoint it is already on rather than falling silent,
// because silence on this channel is what the driver's dead-man measures.
//
// One publisher is not a preference: an input fed by a channel two cogs publish
// to needs a dial the composer will not load a system holding. The session's own
// traffic to the driver therefore has a channel of its own, `SessionCmdChan`.
channel DriverCmd
{
    message_type: Tachyon<goal_vocab::GoalSetpoint>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// What the session has asked for, republished whole on every change. One
// publisher, which is the session cog: an engagement beginning, a session
// ending, and every other change to what the machine is under command to do.
//
// Four deep against a view of one. A schedule is state rather than an event --
// the newest message is the whole of what a consumer needs -- so a subscriber
// lapped here has lost nothing it would have acted on.
channel ScheduleChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<schedule::SessionSchedule>;
    max_num_messages: 4;
}

// What the tick had to say about a cycle: one message per raise, never a
// repetition of a standing condition. One publisher -- the decision tick.
//
// Eight deep against a view of four, which is the session's: the raises are its
// fault evidence as well as the log's. Reports are events rather than cycles, so
// the depth is slack for a burst of them and not a rate.
channel TickFaults
{
    message_type: Tachyon<faults::TickFault>;
    max_num_messages: 8;
}

// The scripts somebody asks the machine to run. One publisher: whatever stands
// at the edge -- the input log in the simulated system, the wake cog online
// until the intent bridge that replaces it lands.
//
// Eight deep against a view of four. Scripts are sparse: one arrives, the
// session runs it for a minute, another arrives. The depth is slack for a
// sender that queued a few at once, and nothing here is a rate.
channel ScriptsIn
{
    message_type: Tachyon<script_vocab::Script>;
    max_num_messages: 8;
}

// What the session says happened: the whole story so far, republished whenever
// a row is added to it. One publisher -- the session.
//
// The channel carries the session's own timeline rows rather than a form shaped
// for the wire: a row is the report, and a second form of it would be something
// to hold in step for nothing.
//
// Cumulative because the reader that matters here is a log, and a logger
// attaches whenever it starts: a row published as its own message before that
// instant is a row nobody can read afterwards. The newest message is the whole
// account, so there is no head to lose.
//
// Four deep against a view of one. The story is state rather than an event --
// the newest message is the whole of it -- so a subscriber lapped here has lost
// nothing it would have acted on.
channel ReportsOut
{
    message_type: Tachyon<timeline::Timeline>;
    max_num_messages: 4;
}

// The one thing the session says to the driver: keep the dead-man fed, take
// torque off now, or run this transaction. One publisher -- the session.
//
// Sixteen deep against a view of eight. The session publishes at most one of
// these per wake and wakes at most ten times a second, so the depth is slack by
// two orders of magnitude and is sized for the loss this channel will suffer
// when it is a socket rather than for the rate it carries.
channel SessionCmdChan
{
    message_type: Tachyon<session_cmd::SessionCmd>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// How one aux transaction turned out. One publisher -- the driver.
//
// Eight deep against a view of four. The host that issues these is serial by
// construction, so there is at most one outstanding request and at most one
// answer per driver cycle; the depth carries a burst of refusals a host that is
// not serial would provoke.
channel DriverAuxOut
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::AuxOutcome>;
    max_num_messages: 8;
}

// What the driver's rotating read of the status registers saw, one report per
// servo visited. One publisher -- the driver.
//
// Sixteen deep against a view of eight. A report every 120 ms is one every six
// bus cycles, so a consumer woken at the session's own floor sees two of them a
// wake and the depth is slack for the burst a stalled consumer comes back to.
channel DriverHealth
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::HealthReport>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// What the driver did that the sample stream does not show: the edges. One
// publisher -- the driver.
//
// Declared here rather than beside the simulated driver because it is driver
// contract and not a fact about the simulator: the session reads it as evidence
// about the machine in this composition and in the online one, and the real
// driver publishes the same records.
//
// Sixteen deep against a view of eight: events mark boundaries, not cycles, and
// a driver emitting one per cycle is a driver reporting a standing condition at
// poll rate.
channel DriverEvt
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverEvent>;
    max_num_messages: 16;
}

// Everything the driver knows about its own run, republished on a cadence and
// cumulative: the newest message is the whole account of the run so far. One
// publisher -- the driver.
//
// Nothing in either system reads it; like `ReportsOut` it exists to be logged,
// and it exists because the driver is outside Clockwork and cannot retain
// anything on a channel for a reader that arrives late. What it carries a
// reader gets whole from whichever copy it saw first.
//
// Eight deep against no view at all: a record a second, and the depth is slack
// for a logger that was busy rather than a rate anything consumes.
channel DriverStatus
{
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverStatus>;
    max_num_messages: 8;
}

// Forward kinematics, once per sample.
//
// The estimator holds no opinion about what the machine should do and issues no
// command; it says where the head is and how well it knows. Its one piece of
// memory is the seed the solver starts from, which is what selects the branch
// of the mechanism the answer lies on -- the mechanism closes in more than one
// configuration and only one of them is the machine.
//
// Each of the three counters below is the run's total. They are held in the
// state slot and reported on the signal group only on the executions where one
// of them moves. Kept in the group's own fold instead they would restart every
// time the group reported, and it would report soonest during an outage that
// moves one of them on every execution -- so the number a reader trusted least
// would be the one it got during the failure it was watching for.
//
// The `simulation_options` at the foot say how long an execution is modelled to
// take. Under the deterministic runner an output is published at the
// execution's start time plus that duration, so an estimate lands in the log
// one millisecond after the sample that produced it, and a checker reading
// timestamps has to know that its `time_of_validity` and its log time are two
// different clocks. It is stated rather than left to the generator's default,
// which happens to be the same millisecond: an assertion about a number needs
// somewhere to read the number from.
cog Pose
{
    states
    {
        // The solver's seed, and the run's totals for the three signals below.
        est: Tappy<pose_state_vocab::PoseState>
        {
            mutable: true;
        }
    }

    signals stats
    {
        // How many solves failed to find a pose. Rises only on a complete
        // sample the solver could not answer -- an incomplete sample is not a
        // failure of the solver, and is visible as an invalid estimate.
        fk_failures: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many samples failed validation. Distinct from a solve failure:
        // the bytes describe no sample at all, so they name no instant and no
        // positions. An execution that refused every message in its window
        // still publishes an invalid estimate, at its own instant; this counter
        // is what says why, and separates a driver writing a field this build
        // does not know from a machine that stopped answering.
        refused_samples: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many times the seed slot held numbers that were not a pose. This
        // cog is the only thing that writes that slot, so a rise means memory
        // nobody wrote was read back as a rotation -- refused, and the solve
        // started from the neutral pose as it does before any answer at all.
        refused_seeds: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }
    }

    inputs
    {
        // The sample stream, sized to see every message rather than the latest
        // one.
        sample: Tappy<pose_vocab::PoseSample>
        {
            max_msgs: 8;
        }
    }

    outputs
    {
        estimate: Tappy<pose_vocab::PoseEstimate>;
    }

    execution
    {
        // Every sample, no skips: the bound equals the view's size, so the
        // count is the window's length and never a clamp of it.
        condition got: new_message(sample, max=8);

        execute when: got;
    }

    simulation_options
    {
        execution_duration: 1ms;
    }
}

// The window a post-aggregated group folds over before it reports. It bounds
// observations, not time: these counters are written only when one of them
// moves. A standing condition can move one of them on every execution -- a
// driver whose datagrams this build refuses does exactly that -- so the window
// can roll at the sample rate, and the size below is what makes that survivable
// rather than what prevents it: every observation is an absolute total, so the
// first report of a fresh window carries the run's count and not the window's.
policy ReportGroupPolicy for Pose.stats
{
    reporting_strategy = ReportingStrategy::post_aggregated;
    log_type = ReportGroupLogType::event;
    max_observations = 1000;
}

// The decision tick: what the machine is asked to do next, once per sample.
//
// This cog is the motion library hosted, not re-implemented. Which goal a
// posture change produces, what counts as an obstruction, when a move has left
// the envelope -- all of that is `reachy-motion`'s tick, called once per sample
// with the state it left last time. What is written here is the hosting: arming
// off a measured pose, reading a session's schedule, turning what the tick
// reported into messages, and keeping the goal stream alive.
//
// The goal stream is a keep-alive. A machine that has arrived and is holding
// re-publishes the setpoint it is on with every sample, because the driver's
// dead-man measures silence on that channel and a holding session is not a
// stopped one. The stream stops on exactly one thing: a fault the tick latches
// on, after which the tick commands nothing and the dead-man de-torques the
// machine -- which is the minimum risk condition arriving by the shortest path
// this system has, not a failure of it.
//
// A report goes out only when something was raised, never on the cycles a
// standing condition is merely still true: a channel carrying a fault at the
// sample rate is a channel nobody can read. One output slot carries one message
// per execution, so a cycle raising two reports publishes the first and counts
// the rest.
//
// The counters below are the run's totals, held in the state slot and reported
// on the signal group only on the executions where one of them moves. Kept in
// the group's own fold instead they would restart every time the group
// reported.
//
// The `simulation_options` at the foot say how long an execution is modelled to
// take: a goal message is logged that long after the sample that decided it,
// while the instant the goal names is arithmetic off the sample's own nominal.
// A checker reading timestamps must not conflate the two. (Neither a block
// header nor a field inside one may carry a comment of its own, which is why
// this is here.)
cog Mover
{
    configs
    {
        // The grid goals are dated on, and how long a posture change takes.
        params: Tappy<config::MoverParams>;

        // Every clip an overlay can name, read-only. A clip's id is its index
        // here, and nothing writes this: clips are chosen before the run
        // starts, so they arrive the way the tick's own numbers do.
        clips: Tappy<config::ClipLibraryConfig>;
    }

    states
    {
        // The tick's own state, and what this cog knows about the schedule it
        // is following.
        ctrl: Tappy<mover_vocab::MoverState>
        {
            mutable: true;
        }
    }

    signals stats
    {
        // How many goals went out. One per execution at most, so under
        // the deterministic runner this is one per sample of an engaged
        // session.
        goals_published: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many reports the tick raised: faults, degrades, abandoned moves
        // and refused commands. Raises, not standing conditions.
        faults_raised: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many samples were read and ticked on.
        samples_seen: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many samples failed validation. A driver writing a field this
        // build does not know is a machine that looks stopped from here; this
        // counter is what tells the two apart.
        refused_samples: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many raises lost the one report slot an execution has.
        reports_dropped: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many times the tick state in this cog's own slot could not be
        // read back as a machine, or would not go back into it. This cog is the
        // only writer of that slot, so a rise means memory nobody wrote was
        // read as a machine, or a machine would not fit the slot it came out
        // of; either way the engagement ended and the next sample starts a new
        // one, which is a session nobody asked to restart.
        refused_state: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many times an epoch this cog had not answered yet was answered by
        // a posture step. Counted per epoch value the cog saw, and the schedule
        // channel is latest-wins, so bumps superseded before an execution are
        // never seen and never counted -- this is a run total, not a subtraction
        // against how many epochs a session published. Whether the epoch
        // published now has been answered is `schedule_epoch_seen` against it.
        epochs_answered: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many overlay windows this cog would not play: the screen's
        // refusals, counted once per schedule, plus each composed setpoint an
        // overlay rode that the tick refused. An overlay is presence, never
        // safety, so a rise is a run that played less than it was asked to and
        // never a run that moved somewhere it should not have.
        overlays_refused: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many overlay rows in this cog's own slot would not read back as a
        // player of the motion their window names. The window plays on from a
        // fresh join, so a rise is a seam in a motion rather than a lost one.
        players_refused: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many base plans this cog could not make or could not read back.
        // The base stands where it stands, so a rise says the machine stopped
        // going where the schedule asked while still holding a setpoint.
        refused_base: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many base plans the motion library adjusted for a reason that is
        // not routine: a clock that could not carry its own span inside the
        // per-tick step bounds, or an antenna pair it could not part. The
        // configured clocks are policy for every span they carry; a rise says
        // they are being asked to cover spans they were not sized for, or that
        // a pair is crossing nearer than the geometry allows.
        base_stretched: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many base plans were adjusted only to part the antenna pair at
        // their crossing. Expected nonzero on any machine that stands up or
        // folds: the pair sweeps mirrored between the stow and the working
        // posture, and the later side's clock is lengthened until their tips
        // clear each other.
        base_dephased: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }
    }

    inputs
    {
        // The driver's sample stream, sized to see every message rather than
        // the latest one: a sample missed is a control cycle that never
        // happened, and this cog's whole clock is that stream.
        sample: Tappy<pose_vocab::PoseSample>
        {
            max_msgs: 8;
        }

        // The session's schedule, at the default view of 1. Latest wins: the
        // message is the whole schedule every time, so the newest one is all
        // there is to know. Deliberately not a condition -- a schedule arriving
        // between samples changes what the next sample decides, not when this
        // cog runs.
        sched: Tappy<schedule::SessionSchedule>;

        // This cog's own goal channel, at the default view of 1: the last
        // setpoint it published, or an empty view before it ever has. The
        // instant that one named, which the next publish is checked against, is
        // read back from it rather than kept in a state field. Deliberately not
        // a condition -- a cog woken by its own publication would never stop.
        own_cmd: Tappy<goal_vocab::GoalSetpoint>;
    }

    outputs
    {
        goal: Tappy<goal_vocab::GoalSetpoint>;
        fault: Tappy<faults::TickFault>;
    }

    execution
    {
        // Every sample, no skips: the bound equals the view's size, so the
        // count is the window's length and never a clamp of it.
        condition got: new_message(sample, max=8);

        execute when: got;
    }

    simulation_options
    {
        execution_duration: 1ms;
    }
}

// The window a post-aggregated group folds over before it reports. It bounds
// observations, not time: these counters are written only when one of them
// moves, and every observation is an absolute total, so the first report of a
// fresh window carries the run's count and not the window's.
policy ReportGroupPolicy for Mover.stats
{
    reporting_strategy = ReportingStrategy::post_aggregated;
    log_type = ReportGroupLogType::event;
    max_observations = 1000;
}

// The session: what the machine is doing at all, and what it says about it.
//
// This cog is the machine's own account of a session -- which phase it is in,
// what script it accepted, what it has said. It decides at human rates and not
// at the bus cycle's, which is why it is woken by the message classes it acts on
// and by a lapse rather than by the sample stream: fifty wakes a second to
// answer nothing is what making that stream a condition would cost. The lapse is
// a floor rather than a period -- what it guarantees is that a wake happens even
// when nothing arrives, which is what a decision about time having passed needs.
//
// Script intake is all-or-nothing: what arrives is screened whole against the
// phase and its own numbers, and either becomes the schedule this session runs
// or is refused with one reason. A refusal is a report and never a fault --
// nothing about the machine is wrong when it declines a script it cannot run.
//
// Reports leave at one per execution, oldest first, out of the ring the state
// slot holds. That ring is what carries a burst across the executions that drain
// it, and it is bounded: a story lost to overflow is narration, because the
// classification that mattered already travelled on the channel that raised it.
//
// The counters below are the run's totals, held in the state slot and reported
// on the signal group only on the executions where one of them moves. Kept in
// the group's own fold instead they would restart every time the group reported.
//
// The `simulation_options` at the foot say how long an execution is modelled to
// take, which is the offset between the wake and the log time of the report it
// publishes. (Neither a block header nor a field inside one may carry a comment
// of its own, which is why this is here.)
cog Session
{
    configs
    {
        // The aux timeout and its retry budget, the two watchdog windows, and
        // the two budgets a response runs on.
        params: Tappy<config::SessionParams>;
    }

    states
    {
        // The phase, the schedule the session accepted, the run's totals and
        // the report ring the narration leaves through.
        sess: Tappy<session_vocab::SessionState>
        {
            mutable: true;
        }
    }

    signals stats
    {
        // How many scripts passed the screen, all run.
        scripts_accepted: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many were refused whole. Every refusal is also a report naming its
        // reason; this is the total a reader compares those against.
        scripts_refused: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many faults were recorded, out of all three kinds of evidence: the
        // decision tick's raises, the edges the driver reported, and the error
        // bits a health reading carried. A raise that asks for no response is
        // not one of these: it is a remark about a command, already on the
        // channel that carried it, and narrating it again would make the
        // timeline a second copy of that channel.
        faults_recorded: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many story datagrams went out: one per execution that added a
        // row, each carrying the whole story.
        reports_published: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many reports were appended to the timeline. The rows a story
        // datagram carries plus the ones it says were dropped.
        reports_narrated: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many reports were dropped off the front of a full ring. A rise
        // says narration was lost, never a record.
        reports_dropped: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many responses were selected. Fewer than the faults recorded: a
        // fault arriving while the response it calls for is already being
        // carried out is answered by the standing one rather than by a second.
        responses_taken: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many inbound datagrams the codec refused -- scripts, fault raises,
        // driver events, aux outcomes, health readings and samples together. A
        // sender built against a newer vocabulary than this binary would
        // otherwise read as a sender that never spoke.
        undecodable_inbound: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many times this cog's own slot did not read back as a session.
        // This cog is the only writer, so a rise means memory nobody wrote was
        // read as a session.
        refused_state: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many datagrams were re-issued because nothing answered the first
        // one inside the configured window. Delivery retry: the same datagram
        // under the same correlation number, so a rise is a lossy link or a
        // driver that is late, never a second ask.
        aux_retries: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many transactions were given up on with the retry budget spent.
        // Each one is handed to the sequencer as a silence, which is what fails
        // the sequence and hands the phase machine a refusal.
        aux_failures: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }

        // How many answers named a number nothing was waiting on, and were
        // dropped.
        aux_strays: signal UInt64
        {
            post_aggregation: ["final_value"];
            multi_instance: true;
        }
    }

    inputs
    {
        // The scripts, sized to see every one rather than the latest: a script
        // is a request and a request superseded before it was read is a request
        // nobody answered.
        script: Tappy<script_vocab::Script>
        {
            max_msgs: 4;
        }

        // What the decision tick raised, sized to see every raise for the same
        // reason: a raise is evidence about the machine, and evidence
        // superseded before it was read is evidence nobody weighed.
        fault: Tappy<faults::TickFault>
        {
            max_msgs: 4;
        }

        // The driver's answers to the transactions this cog issued. One request
        // is outstanding at a time, so the window only ever holds a late
        // duplicate beside the answer being waited on -- and both are read,
        // because the correlation number is what tells them apart.
        aux_out: Tappy<health::AuxOutcome>
        {
            max_msgs: 4;
        }

        // What the driver did that the pose stream does not show: a de-torquing
        // it latched, a confirmation it read back, a bus it declared gone. Every
        // one of them, because each is an edge and an edge nobody read is an
        // edge that did not happen as far as this cog is concerned.
        evt: Tappy<health::DriverEvent>
        {
            max_msgs: 8;
        }

        // What the driver's rotating read saw, one servo at a time. Sized to
        // hold a whole sweep of the bus: the error bits a reading carries are
        // evidence about that servo alone, so a reading dropped is a servo this
        // cog never heard about. Named for the readings rather than for the
        // health they are about, because a member named `health` shadows the
        // module of that name and the compiler refuses it.
        readings: Tappy<health::HealthReport>
        {
            max_msgs: 8;
        }

        // The driver's sample stream, read and never triggering: it arrives at
        // the bus rate and this cog runs at the rate of the decisions it makes,
        // so what a wake wants from it is the freshest instant rather than every
        // one of them. Which is also all it is read for, on both of the
        // questions it answers: whether the driver is still alive, and -- for a
        // session that has not commissioned the machine yet -- whether it ever
        // was. Nothing is asked of the bus until a sample says a driver is
        // cycling to answer it.
        sample: Tappy<pose_vocab::PoseSample>;
    }

    outputs
    {
        // What the driver is asked for: one datagram per execution at most,
        // which is what makes a keep-alive unable to displace a transaction.
        cmd: Tappy<session_cmd::SessionCmd>;

        // What the machine is under command to do, whole: published when the
        // engagement takes hold and when what it was engaged for is over, and
        // never between changes. A consumer reads the latest and needs no
        // history, so one message per change is the whole contract.
        sched: Tappy<schedule::SessionSchedule>;

        // The whole story so far, published on every execution that added a row
        // to it and on no other: a message is the account of the run, so a
        // reader that saw only the newest one has missed nothing.
        report: Tappy<timeline::Timeline>;
    }

    execution
    {
        // Every script, no skips: the bound equals the view's size, so the count
        // is the window's length and never a clamp of it.
        condition got_script: new_message(script, max=4);

        // Every raise, likewise.
        condition got_fault: new_message(fault, max=4);

        // Every answer. A sequence advances one transaction per answer, so this
        // is what paces a commission: the wake floor below is a deadline for
        // the answer that never came, not the rate the bus is driven at.
        condition got_aux: new_message(aux_out, max=4);

        // Every edge the driver reported, and every reading its rotation took.
        // Both are fault evidence, and evidence waits for no floor: a servo that
        // has just reported a hardware error is answered on the wake its reading
        // causes.
        condition got_evt: new_message(evt, max=8);
        condition got_readings: new_message(readings, max=8);

        // The floor. A wake owes the machine a decision about time having
        // passed, and this is what guarantees one happens even when nothing
        // arrives.
        condition lapse: time_since_last_exec(100ms);

        execute when: got_script or got_fault or got_aux or got_evt or got_readings or lapse;
    }

    simulation_options
    {
        execution_duration: 1ms;
    }
}

// The window a post-aggregated group folds over before it reports. It bounds
// observations, not time: these counters are written only when one of them
// moves, and every observation is an absolute total, so the first report of a
// fresh window carries the run's count and not the window's.
policy ReportGroupPolicy for Session.stats
{
    reporting_strategy = ReportingStrategy::post_aggregated;
    log_type = ReportGroupLogType::event;
    max_observations = 1000;
}

// The control loop without its driver: the decision tick, the estimator, the
// session, their state, their configuration, and every edge between a channel
// above and one of the three.
//
// It is a box rather than three `new` lines in each system because the two
// systems differ in exactly one thing -- what stands at the driver's end of the
// wire. The simulated system puts a cog there; the one that runs on a machine
// puts sockets to a driver process there. Everything else is the same topology,
// and a topology stated twice is a topology that drifts. So the core is stated
// here and the driver's half is drawn in whichever composition encloses it: this
// box deliberately leaves the driver-side ends of `DriverPose`, `DriverCmd`,
// `SessionCmdChan`, `DriverEvt`, `DriverAuxOut` and `DriverHealth` unconnected,
// and an enclosing box that does not fill them is a system with no driver.
//
// Channels are module-scope and identified globally, so an edge drawn here and
// an edge drawn in an outer box are edges on the same channel.
box MotionCore
{
    new mover: Mover;
    new pose: Pose;
    new session: Session;

    // Each cog's own slot, written by nothing else. No `init=`, so each is
    // default-constructed into a buffer the compiler synthesizes.
    new mover_state: State(representation=Tachyon<mover_vocab::MoverState>);
    connect mover_state to mover.ctrl;

    new pose_state: State(representation=Tachyon<pose_state_vocab::PoseState>);
    connect pose_state to pose.est;

    new session_state: State(representation=Tachyon<session_vocab::SessionState>);
    connect session_state to session.sess;

    // The configs, read from checked-in protobuf text before the first
    // execution. The paths resolve against the runfiles of whatever runs the
    // process, which is why the files are data dependencies of the executable.
    new mover_params: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<config::MoverParams>,
        path="cogs/mover_params.textproto");
    connect mover_params to mover.params;

    new session_params: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<config::SessionParams>,
        path="cogs/session_params.textproto");
    connect session_params to session.params;

    // The clips an overlay can name. Emitted from the documents under
    // `cogs/clips/` rather than authored, and read the same way the two
    // parameter files are.
    new clip_library: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<config::ClipLibraryConfig>,
        path="cogs/clip_library.textproto");
    connect clip_library to mover.clips;

    // The sample stream: whatever is at the driver's end publishes it, both
    // control-rate cogs run on it, and the session reads it for one thing only
    // -- whether the driver is still publishing at all, which a driver cannot
    // report about itself -- so it takes the freshest instant and is never woken
    // by one.
    connect DriverPose to mover.sample;
    connect DriverPose to pose.sample;
    connect DriverPose to session.sample;

    // The goal stream, and the loop it closes: the tick commands, the driver's
    // gate decides what is due, and the tick reads back what it last sent.
    connect mover.goal to DriverCmd;
    connect DriverCmd to mover.own_cmd;

    // What the run is asked to do. The publisher is outside this box: an input
    // log in the simulated system, an intent source on a machine.
    connect ScriptsIn to session.script;

    // What the session accepted, reaching the cog that carries it out. The
    // session is the channel's one publisher, so the schedule the mover streams
    // is the schedule a script was screened into.
    connect session.sched to ScheduleChan;
    connect ScheduleChan to mover.sched;

    // What the tick raised: the log's record of it, and the session's evidence
    // about the machine.
    connect mover.fault to TickFaults;
    connect TickFaults to session.fault;

    // What the session asks of the driver, and what the driver answers. The
    // request channel's one publisher is the session.
    connect session.cmd to SessionCmdChan;
    connect DriverAuxOut to session.aux_out;

    // What the driver says about itself, and the session weighing it: an edge
    // the driver reported and a servo's error bits are the two kinds of fault
    // evidence the decision tick cannot see.
    connect DriverEvt to session.evt;
    connect DriverHealth to session.readings;

    // Logged only: nothing in either system consumes either.
    connect pose.estimate to Estimates;
    connect session.report to ReportsOut;
}
